<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Somebody else's library, kept in this tree instead of restored from NuGet.

    BBCodeParser 1.0.0 is the only release the package ever had (April 2018). It
    carries no licence expression and no repository link, so the binary on
    nuget.org is the whole of it: nothing to read, nothing to fix, nothing to
    build from. The sources are at github.com/quilin/BBCodeParser under MIT, and
    the author is the previous developer of this project - the package is an
    artifact of this codebase that happened to be published, which is why its
    assembly still names dm.am as the company.

    Which state was taken, and why it is not the default branch: the branch does
    not compile. Two commits of March 2018 changed Tags/Tag.cs to call
    BbParser.AttributeSecuritySubstitutions, a member the committed BbParser.cs
    never gained. The shipped 1.0.0 assembly does not contain that member either,
    and its escape pattern is the one from before those commits, so the state the
    package was built from is commit c2ccefdf9d - the last commit at which the
    repository is both complete and equal to the binary this solution was
    compiling against. Every file here matches the default branch except
    Tags/Tag.cs, which comes from that commit.

    Its own assembly rather than a folder inside DM.Infrastructure.Core, for two
    reasons. The container scans that assembly and registers what it finds by
    implemented interface; BbParser implements IBbParser, which is the interface
    BbParserProvider hands out, so the scan would have a second answer for it.
    And the nullable policy below has to be scoped to code nobody here wrote,
    which a project can do and a folder cannot.

    The assembly name is unchanged on purpose: consumers keep the same using
    directives and the same fully qualified names, so the move from a package
    reference to a project reference is the only thing that happened.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <!--
      The solution compiles under nullable annotations; this code predates them
      by years and expresses "no filter" as a null default on a delegate
      parameter. Annotating it would be a rewrite of somebody else's library
      under a policy it never agreed to, and every annotation is a chance to
      change what the parser does to text that is already stored. Off for this
      project only - consumers see the same oblivious API the package published,
      so nothing about their own analysis changes.
    -->
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
